Z||000000||dgroves||09-20-2001||06:12 PM||||When changing from a 2.8 to a 3.1, is it OK to just drill the spot welds off the flexplate that hold the balance weights? That should make it neutural. Has anybody done this in liew of the '88 flexplate?||||reg||5
Z||000001||dgroves||09-21-2001||07:18 PM||||Come on guys. Surely sombody has adapted the flexplate from a 2.8 to a 3.1. ||||reg||2
Z||000002||BC-GT||09-21-2001||08:34 PM||||Do a search for Ironhead3 or IronHedNo3, I believe he talked about whacking the weight off to become neutral balanced. I would definitely want to get it balanced after that though. Have not done it myself.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.imagestation.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||California Kid||09-21-2001||09:23 PM||||If you're sure the engine in internally balanced, remove the weight from the flex plate. If you want to be a real "purest" you can have the flex plate balanced true neutral, but this isn't really required.<P>I had a new 88 2.8 put in my '87 GT and they forgot to remove the weight from the flex plate. It does set up a vibration in the engine, most noticable at idle. Now I have to figure out if I can get at the weight to remove it without removing the trans.||||reg||1
